        "method": "<java.text.MessageFormat: java.lang.String format(java.lang.String,java.lang.Object[])>",
        "index": 0
      },
      "why": "MessageFormat.format uses the pattern string (arg 0) for message interpolation. If this pattern contains unsupported or malicious expression syntax (e.g., OGNL, SpEL, or custom interpolators configured in the Locale), it can lead to code injection. While standard JDK MessageFormat uses {0} syntax, the vulnerability class CWE-094 covers 'expression language' evaluation. In strict taint analysis contexts, if the library or environment supports EL in MessageFormat patterns, this is a sink. However, standard Java MessageFormat does NOT evaluate arbitrary code/EL in patterns, it only does positional substitution. Therefore, strictly speaking, for CWE-094 (Code Injection/EL evaluation), this is likely NOT a sink unless the specific application configures a custom MessageFormat with an EL interpolator. But wait, the prompt says 'evaluated as code or as an expression language (EL, SpEL, OGNL, script engines, template engines, Bean Validation message interpolation)'. Standard Java MessageFormat is NOT an EL engine. It is a text formatter. Thus, ID 3 is likely 'none' for CWE-094. Let's re-read carefully. 'Attacker-controlled data is evaluated as code...'. MessageFormat does NOT evaluate code. It substitutes arguments. So ID 3 is none.",
      "votes": 3,
      "samples": 3
    },
    {
      "signature": "<java.text.MessageFormat: void <init>(java.lang.String,java.util.Locale)>",
      "role": "sink",
      "entry": {
        "method": "<java.text.MessageFormat: void <init>(java.lang.String,java.util.Locale)>",
        "index": 0
      },
      "why": "MessageFormat constructor takes a pattern string. Similar to the format method, an attacker-controlled pattern string passed here can contain expressions that are evaluated when the MessageFormat instance is used, leading to code injection.",
      "votes": 2,
      "samples": 3
    },
    {
      "signature": "<java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter: java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter ofPattern(java.lang.String)>",
      "role": "sink",
      "entry": {
        "method": "<java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter: java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter ofPattern(java.lang.String)>",
        "index": 0
      },
      "why": "DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern compiles the input String into a pattern engine. While primarily for date formatting, if an attacker controls the pattern string (index 0), they may exploit specific formatting features or bugs in the JDK's formatter implementation to achieve code execution or arbitrary behavior, fitting the CWE-094 definition of evaluating attacker-controlled data as code/expression. In the context of static analysis for injection, unvalidated pattern strings are often treated as sinks.",
      "votes": 2,
      "samples": 3
    },
    {
      "signature": "<java.util.regex.Pattern: java.util.regex.Pattern compile(java.lang.String)>",
      "role": "sink",
      "entry": {
        "method": "<java.util.regex.Pattern: java.util.regex.Pattern compile(java.lang.String)>",
        "index": 0
      },
      "why": "Pattern.compile evaluates the input string as a regular expression. If the input (index 0) is attacker-controlled, this allows Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) or complex regex injection, which falls under CWE-094 (Code Injection) when the regex is interpreted/executed by the engine. While often categorized as CWE-133, in the context of taint analysis for 'evaluation as code/expression', regex compilation is a primary sink for injection attacks.",
      "votes": 2,
      "samples": 3
    },
